\title{
    \vspace{-2em}
    \textbf{\Large AXON-RFC-006 Appendix B (v0.5.1):}\\[0.2em]
    \textbf{\Large \texttt{easynet pages} --- publishing a folder as a website}\\[0.4em]
    \large User-owned projects, byte-addressing resource URAs,\\
    \large kernel-enforced root sandbox\\[0.2em]
    \large MVP: \texttt{hello-world.html + style.css}
}
\author{
    Silan Hu \\ \small National University of Singapore
}
\date{Draft v0.5.1, \today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent
A user publishes a folder. EasyNet mints a URA scheme that
addresses every public byte in the folder: the URA tail is
\texttt{<user-id>.<project-id>/<path>}, where the dot-joined
prefix denotes user-and-project identity and the slash-suffixed
path denotes the byte's location inside the project's root.
The folder root is the project's hard sandbox boundary --- the
fetch path runs inside a kernel-enforced resolver
(\texttt{RESOLVE\_BENEATH}) that cannot escape the published
root, no matter what symlinks or \texttt{..} components a
caller supplies. One ability per project is registered
(\texttt{<user-id>.<project-id>.page.fetch}); the Hub serves
HTTP requests on \texttt{<project-id>.<user-id>.pages.<realm>}
by \texttt{forward\_invoke}-ing that ability with the requested
path. The minimum deliverable is two files,
\texttt{hello-world.html} and \texttt{style.css}, served
through a real browser fetch and a kernel-enforced sandbox.
v0.5.1 is the addressing form; everything bigger (SPA, API,
peer actions, federation, SCOPED visibility) is deliberate
post-MVP.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents
\vspace{1em}
\hrule
\vspace{1em}

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{What changed from v0.5}
\label{sec:v05-diff}

v0.5 routed the read path through a project-level resource
(\texttt{resource/pages.<rid>}) and made fetch take \texttt{path}
as an argument. The two CTO clarifications collapsed the
addressing form:

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{l X}
\toprule
\textbf{v0.5} & \textbf{v0.5.1} \\
\midrule
URA tail was a single dot-flat string (\texttt{pages.<rid>}; tail used dots only)
 & URA tail is \texttt{<user-id>.<project-id>/<path>} --- dot-joined identity and slash-joined byte path. The tail directly addresses a public byte stream. \\
First segment of the ability/resource tail was an opaque rid
 & First segment is the publishing user's id (alice, bob, \dots) --- a user-id, not an agent-id. Projects are user-owned. \\
Resource URA was project-scoped; files were args
 & Resource URA addresses bytes. \texttt{index.html}, \texttt{style.css}, \texttt{assets/logo.png} each carry a distinct resource URA. \\
Ownership scope of an ability was implicitly ``agent or resource''
 & Ability ownership extends to \texttt{(user \(\mid\) agent \(\mid\) hub \(\mid\) resource)}; this RFC roots the page-fetch ability at the user, namespaced by the project. \\
URL was \texttt{<rid>.pages.<realm>}
 & URL is \texttt{<project-id>.<user-id>.pages.<realm>/<path>}, mirroring URA tail one-to-one (subdomain holds project + user; path holds the byte path verbatim). \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}

\noindent The number of abilities registered is unchanged
(one per project). The change is in addressing, not in
ability proliferation.

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{The URA shape}
\label{sec:ura}

\subsection{Tail grammar}

\normsv{} URA v4.1.5 §A.URA-7 fixes the URA shape at three
segments:

\begin{lstlisting}
easynet:///r/<realm>/<role>/<tail>
\end{lstlisting}

Within \texttt{<tail>}, this RFC adopts a two-part grammar:

\begin{lstlisting}
<tail> = <id-part> ( "/" <path-part> )?
<id-part>   = <segment> ( "." <segment> )*           dot-joined
<path-part> = <segment> ( "/" <segment> )*           slash-joined
<segment>   = [a-zA-Z0-9_-]+
\end{lstlisting}

The id part denotes ownership and naming; the path part denotes
a byte address inside whatever the id part identifies. Both
parts are case-sensitive. Either part may be empty, but the
slash separator only appears when both are non-empty.

\begin{notebox}
\textbf{This grammar is consistent with v4.1.5.} v4.1.5 §A.URA-7
constrains the URA at the segment count of three, not the
internal structure of the tail. The tail in this RFC is a
single segment in the URA-segment sense; the dot/slash split is
a profile-defined decomposition that the parser presents as
\texttt{(id\_part, path\_part)}.
\end{notebox}

\subsection{Resource URA: byte addressing}

\normsv{} A published folder yields one resource URA per public
byte stream:

\begin{lstlisting}
easynet:///r/<realm>/resource/<user-id>.<project-id>/<path>
                              └─── id-part ────────┘└─path─┘
\end{lstlisting}

For the running example:

\begin{lstlisting}
easynet:///r/easynet.run/resource/alice.papers/index.html
easynet:///r/easynet.run/resource/alice.papers/style.css
easynet:///r/easynet.run/resource/alice.papers/assets/logo.png
\end{lstlisting}

The \texttt{<path>} component mirrors the file's location under
the published folder root (the publish target). It is verbatim
filesystem path, with the leading slash present.

\begin{invariant}[PG-INV-1 (root sandbox)]
The path component of any \texttt{resource/<user-id>.<project-id>/<path>}
URA \textbf{MUST} resolve to a regular file beneath the
published folder root. Symbolic links, \texttt{..} traversal,
and any other resolution that escapes the root are
non-conformant. The daemon enforces this through a kernel
contract (\S\ref{sec:sandbox}); it is not a user-space check.
\end{invariant}

\subsection{Ability URA: one per project}

\normsv{} Despite each public byte carrying a distinct resource
URA, only one ability is registered per project:

\begin{lstlisting}
easynet:///r/<realm>/ability/<user-id>.<project-id>.page.fetch
                             └─── id-part ─────────────────┘
\end{lstlisting}

For the running example:

\begin{lstlisting}
easynet:///r/easynet.run/ability/alice.papers.page.fetch
\end{lstlisting}

The ability accepts a \texttt{path} argument; one
\texttt{fetch} call returns one byte stream. Registering one
ability per file would multiply registry weight by the file
count without any expressive gain --- the per-file resource URA
already addresses the byte; the ability is the method that
reads it.

\begin{invariant}[PG-INV-2 (one ability per project)]
A project \textbf{MUST} register exactly one fetch ability,
\texttt{ability/<user-id>.<project-id>.page.fetch}. Multiple
fetch abilities (per-file or per-extension) are non-conformant.
\end{invariant}

\subsection{Ability ownership widens}

\derived{} v4.1.5's ability namespace was rooted at agent
identity (\texttt{ability/<agent-id>.<name>}). v0.5.1 widens
the ownership category to \texttt{(user \(\mid\) agent \(\mid\)
hub \(\mid\) resource)}. The widening is an ontology
clarification, not a grammar change: ability tails were always
free-form \texttt{<owner-id>.<name>} pairs; v0.5.1 lifts
\texttt{<owner-id>} to the unifying notion of an ownership
holder rather than the narrower notion of an agent.

\begin{notebox}
The widening composes with the existing ontology cleanly:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.1em]
    \item User-rooted: \texttt{ability/alice.papers.page.fetch} --- the page in this RFC.
    \item Agent-rooted: \texttt{ability/alice.claude.skill.summarize} --- a Claude-Code-hosted skill.
    \item Hub-rooted: \texttt{ability/01HUB.pages.serve} --- the Hub's HTTP boundary (\S\ref{sec:hub}).
    \item Resource-rooted: \texttt{ability/<rid>.method} --- reserved for resources whose execution lives at the resource itself rather than at the user/agent (post-MVP shape).
\end{itemize}
The four roots share grammar; admission distinguishes them by
the URA kind of the owner.
\end{notebox}

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Hub: the only HTTP boundary}
\label{sec:hub}

\subsection{Hub-as-agent owns one ability}

\normsv{} The Hub binds the wildcard host pattern
\texttt{*.*.pages.<realm>} on its TLS listener (two-level
wildcard: project \texttt{.} user \texttt{.} pages \texttt{.}
realm). On any HTTP request matching this pattern, the Hub
invokes:

\begin{lstlisting}
caller   = hub/01HUB
callee   = hub/01HUB
ability  = ability/01HUB.pages.serve
subject  = resource/<user-id>.<project-id>/<path>     // parsed from URL
args     = {
    method:      "GET",
    request_id:  "...",
    user_agent:  "...",
}
\end{lstlisting}

The Hub's \texttt{pages.serve} ability:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.15em]
    \item Parses the host: \texttt{<project-id>.<user-id>.pages.<realm>}.
    \item Constructs the subject URA \texttt{resource/<user-id>.<project-id>/<path>}.
    \item Resolves the host agent for the project through the federation directory.
    \item Issues a \texttt{forward\_invoke} to the host:
\begin{lstlisting}
caller    = hub/01HUB
callee    = agent/<host-of-project>
ability   = ability/<user-id>.<project-id>.page.fetch
subject   = resource/<user-id>.<project-id>/<path>
args      = { path: "<path>" }
\end{lstlisting}
    \item Streams the returned bytes back to the browser with the Content-Type the fetch ability returned.
    \item Emits one operational receipt for the serve.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{invariant}[PG-INV-3 (one Hub boundary)]
HTTP requests for \texttt{*.*.pages.<realm>} \textbf{MUST}
enter EasyNet through the \texttt{ability/01HUB.pages.serve}
call site. There is no private back-channel that lets static
bytes flow without the \texttt{forward\_invoke} round trip.
\end{invariant}

\subsection{URL form}
\label{sec:url}

\normsv{}

\begin{lstlisting}
https://<project-id>.<user-id>.pages.<realm>/<path>
\end{lstlisting}

For the running example:

\begin{lstlisting}
https://papers.alice.pages.easynet.run/index.html
https://papers.alice.pages.easynet.run/style.css
https://papers.alice.pages.easynet.run/assets/logo.png
\end{lstlisting}

URL and URA mirror each other exactly:

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.18}
\begin{tabular}{l l}
\toprule
\textbf{URL} & \textbf{URA} \\
\midrule
\texttt{https://papers.alice.pages.easynet.run/index.html}
 & \texttt{easynet:///r/easynet.run/resource/alice.papers/index.html} \\
\texttt{https://papers.alice.pages.easynet.run/assets/logo.png}
 & \texttt{easynet:///r/easynet.run/resource/alice.papers/assets/logo.png} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The transformation is mechanical:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.1em]
    \item Subdomain \texttt{<project>.<user>} \(\to\) URA id-part \texttt{<user>.<project>} (order reverses; the URL leads with the more-specific component, the URA leads with the owner).
    \item URL path \(\to\) URA path-part verbatim.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{rid: id, alias, content hash --- all interchangeable}

\normsv{} \texttt{<project-id>} can be:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.15em]
    \item A user-chosen human-readable name (\texttt{papers},
    \texttt{shop}, \texttt{notes}). Default for PUBLIC publishes.
    Subject to per-user uniqueness check at publish time.
    \item A content-addressed hash (the first 16 hex characters
    of the folder's Merkle hash). Stable across hosts and
    restarts; collision-free for federation.
    \item An alias --- a separately-minted name pointing at a
    specific content hash. Multiple aliases can resolve to the
    same canonical project; renaming or transferring projects
    is alias bookkeeping.
\end{itemize}

The Hub resolves all three through the federation directory; from
the URL's perspective they are interchangeable. PUBLIC publishes
default to user-chosen names; PRIVATE publishes (post-MVP)
\textbf{MUST} use a 64-hex content hash or a separately-minted
unguessable token.

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Daemon: the read sandbox}
\label{sec:sandbox}

\normsv{} \texttt{ability/<user-id>.<project-id>.page.fetch}
\textbf{MUST} run inside a kernel-enforced read sandbox
rooted at the published folder. User-space path validation
alone is non-conformant.

\subsection{Mechanism (Linux)}

On \texttt{pages.publish}, the daemon opens the folder as a
directory file descriptor and pins it in memory:

\begin{lstlisting}
let folder_fd = open(folder_path, O_DIRECTORY | O_CLOEXEC)?;
PublishedProjects.insert(
    (user_id, project_id),
    ProjectHandle {
        folder_fd,                                 // pinned for the
                                                   // lifetime of the publish
        canonical_root: canonicalize(folder_path)?,
        visibility,
        file_size_cap: 100 * 1024 * 1024,         // 100 MiB
    }
);
\end{lstlisting}

On \texttt{fetch(path)}:

\begin{lstlisting}
let file_fd = openat2(
    handle.folder_fd,
    args.path.trim_start_matches('/'),
    OpenHow {
        flags:   O_RDONLY | O_NOFOLLOW | O_CLOEXEC,
        mode:    0,
        resolve: RESOLVE_BENEATH                    // kernel guarantee:
                | RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS               //   resolution does
                | RESOLVE_NO_MAGICLINKS,            //   not escape folder_fd
    },
)?;
let metadata = fstat(file_fd)?;
require!(metadata.is_regular_file());               // deny FIFO/dev/socket
require!(metadata.size <= handle.file_size_cap);
let bytes = read_to_end(file_fd)?;
let mime  = mime_from_extension(args.path);
return FetchResponse { bytes, content_type: mime };
\end{lstlisting}

\texttt{RESOLVE\_BENEATH} guarantees, at the kernel level,
that path resolution cannot leave the directory the
\texttt{dirfd} was opened on, regardless of \texttt{..}
components, regardless of symlinks, regardless of how the
filesystem mutates between calls.

\begin{invariant}[PG-INV-4 (kernel-enforced sandbox)]
Linux daemons \textbf{MUST} use \texttt{openat2(2)} with
\texttt{RESOLVE\_BENEATH | RESOLVE\_NO\_SYMLINKS |
RESOLVE\_NO\_MAGICLINKS} for every fetch read. The fall-back
to \texttt{realpath()}-then-prefix-check is non-conformant: it
races the filesystem (TOCTOU) and is bypassable.
\end{invariant}

\subsection{Mechanism (macOS)}

\normsv{} macOS lacks \texttt{RESOLVE\_BENEATH}. v0.5.1
mitigates with three layers:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.15em]
    \item \texttt{realpath(3)} on the resolved candidate;
    require \texttt{starts\_with(handle.canonical\_root)}.
    \item \texttt{O\_NOFOLLOW | O\_CLOEXEC} on the open.
    \item Daemon process wrapped in a Mach
    \texttt{sandbox\_init}/\texttt{sandbox-exec} profile
    declaring read-only access to published folders only.
\end{enumerate}

The macOS combination is structurally weaker than the Linux
contract; production targets Linux, and macOS parity (a
per-publish sandboxed worker) is deferred (see
\S\ref{sec:open}).

\subsection{Default-deny content discipline}

\normsv{} Independent of the kernel sandbox, fetch enforces:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.15em]
    \item Files whose name starts with \texttt{.} (dotfiles)
    are refused with 404 (\texttt{.git/}, \texttt{.env},
    \texttt{.DS\_Store}, \dots).
    \item Extensions outside the MIME allow-list are served as
    \texttt{application/octet-stream} with
    \texttt{Content-Disposition: attachment} so the browser
    cannot interpret them as code.
    \item Per-file size cap (default 100 MiB; configurable);
    over-cap returns 502 to the Hub.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{MIME allow-list}

\normsv{} v0.5.1 ships:

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.18}
\begin{tabular}{l l}
\toprule
\textbf{Extension} & \textbf{Content-Type} \\
\midrule
\texttt{.html, .htm}   & \texttt{text/html; charset=utf-8} \\
\texttt{.css}          & \texttt{text/css; charset=utf-8} \\
\texttt{.js, .mjs}     & \texttt{application/javascript; charset=utf-8} \\
\texttt{.json}         & \texttt{application/json; charset=utf-8} \\
\texttt{.svg}          & \texttt{image/svg+xml} \\
\texttt{.png}          & \texttt{image/png} \\
\texttt{.jpg, .jpeg}   & \texttt{image/jpeg} \\
\texttt{.gif}          & \texttt{image/gif} \\
\texttt{.webp}         & \texttt{image/webp} \\
\texttt{.woff, .woff2} & \texttt{font/woff[2]} \\
\texttt{.txt, .md}     & \texttt{text/plain; charset=utf-8} \\
\texttt{(other)}       & \texttt{application/octet-stream} (download) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Receipts: two tiers}
\label{sec:receipts}

\normsv{} v0.5.1 keeps the v0.5 two-tier classification:

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.2}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{l X X}
\toprule
\textbf{Tier} & \textbf{Used for} & \textbf{Storage} \\
\midrule
Canonical
 & \texttt{<user-id>.pages.publish} (state-changing). One receipt per call. Persistent. Walk-chained.
 & The receipt store from URA v2 plan §Phase 6. \\
Operational
 & \texttt{<user-id>.<project-id>.page.fetch}, \texttt{01HUB.pages.serve} (state-preserving, idempotent reads). One receipt per call.
 & Bounded ring buffer (lossy under load); rates surfaced through \texttt{observe.health}. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}

\begin{invariant}[PG-INV-5 (idempotent reads are operational)]
\texttt{<user-id>.<project-id>.page.fetch} and
\texttt{01HUB.pages.serve} \textbf{MUST} be classified as
operational receipts. Forcing read-path receipts onto the
canonical chain saturates it under any realistic browser load.
\end{invariant}

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Minimum deliverable: \texttt{hello-world.html + style.css}}
\label{sec:mvp}

\subsection{Scenario}

The publish folder:

\begin{lstlisting}
/Users/alice/site/
    hello-world.html
    style.css
\end{lstlisting}

Contents:

\begin{lstlisting}
hello-world.html
————————————————————————————————————————————————
<!doctype html>
<html>
  <head>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
    <title>Hello</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Hello, EasyNet.</h1>
  </body>
</html>

style.css
————————————————————————————————————————————————
h1 { color: red; font-family: ui-sans-serif; }
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{End-to-end trace}

\textbf{Publish}:

\begin{lstlisting}
$ easynet ability invoke alice.pages.publish \
    --args '{"folder":"/Users/alice/site",
             "project_id":"papers",
             "visibility":"PUBLIC"}'

→ {
    "project_uri": "easynet:///r/easynet.run/resource/alice.papers",
    "url_root":    "https://papers.alice.pages.easynet.run/"
  }
\end{lstlisting}

Daemon side:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.12em]
    \item \texttt{open("/Users/alice/site", O\_DIRECTORY)}; pins fd.
    \item Inserts \texttt{(alice, papers) \(\to\) ProjectHandle} into in-memory \texttt{PublishedProjects}.
    \item Registers \texttt{ability/alice.papers.page.fetch} into the local ability registry.
    \item Emits canonical receipt: \texttt{ability=ability/alice.pages.publish}, \texttt{subject=user/alice}, \texttt{post=resource/alice.papers/}.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Browser fetch (HTML)}:

\begin{lstlisting}
GET /hello-world.html HTTP/1.1
Host: papers.alice.pages.easynet.run
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent Hub:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.12em]
    \item Subdomain parse: project=\texttt{papers}, user=\texttt{alice}.
    \item Constructs subject: \texttt{resource/alice.papers/hello-world.html}.
    \item Directory lookup \(\to\) host agent for \texttt{alice.papers}.
    \item \texttt{forward\_invoke} \texttt{ability/alice.papers.page.fetch} with \texttt{args=\{path:"/hello-world.html"\}}.
    \item Operational receipt for the serve.
\end{itemize}

\noindent Daemon:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.12em]
    \item \texttt{openat2(folder\_fd, "hello-world.html", RESOLVE\_BENEATH \dots)}.
    \item \texttt{fstat} \(\to\) regular file, 173 bytes.
    \item Read; MIME = \texttt{text/html; charset=utf-8}.
    \item Operational receipt for the fetch.
    \item Returns \texttt{FetchResponse}.
\end{itemize}

\noindent Browser response:

\begin{lstlisting}
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8
Cache-Control: max-age=60
Content-Length: 173

<!doctype html>
<html>
  <head>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
    ...
\end{lstlisting}

\textbf{Browser fetch (CSS)}: identical pattern,
\texttt{path = "/style.css"}, MIME \texttt{text/css}. Browser
parses, applies \texttt{color: red} to the \texttt{h1}.

\textbf{Path-traversal attempt}:

\begin{lstlisting}
GET /../../etc/passwd HTTP/1.1
Host: papers.alice.pages.easynet.run

→ HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent The daemon's \texttt{openat2} returns
\texttt{EXDEV} (or \texttt{ELOOP}, kernel-version dependent)
because path resolution attempted to escape \texttt{folder\_fd}.
The kernel refuses the open; the daemon never reads
\texttt{/etc/passwd}; the Hub renders 404.

\subsection{Test matrix}

\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.18}
\begin{xltabular}{\linewidth}{l l L{3.0cm} L{3.4cm} L{3.6cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Case} & \textbf{L1 (CLI)} & \textbf{L2 (HTTP)} & \textbf{L3 (state)} \\
\midrule
\endfirsthead
\toprule
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Case} & \textbf{L1} & \textbf{L2} & \textbf{L3} \\
\midrule
\endhead
M1 & \texttt{pages.publish} of two-file folder & exit 0; stdout has \texttt{project\_uri} + \texttt{url\_root} & --- & \texttt{PublishedProjects} contains the \texttt{(user,project)} entry; ability registry contains \texttt{ability/alice.papers.page.fetch}; canonical receipt logged \\
M2 & \texttt{GET /hello-world.html} & --- & 200; \texttt{text/html}; body bytes match file & operational receipt logged \\
M3 & \texttt{GET /style.css} & --- & 200; \texttt{text/css}; body bytes match file & operational receipt logged \\
M4 & \texttt{GET /../../etc/passwd} (path traversal) & --- & 404 & daemon never opens the target file; \texttt{openat2} returns \texttt{EXDEV}; no read syscall to \texttt{/etc/passwd} \\
M5 & \texttt{GET /nonexistent.html} & --- & 404 & no successful read \\
M6 & dotfile probe (\texttt{GET /.env} or \texttt{/.git/HEAD}) & --- & 404 & blocked by default-deny dotfile rule \\
M7 & symlink escape (folder contains \texttt{escape \(\to\) /etc/passwd}) & --- & 404 & \texttt{RESOLVE\_NO\_SYMLINKS} blocks open \\
\bottomrule
\end{xltabular}

\noindent A phase ships only when \emph{every} case passes at
all three layers. M4 + M6 + M7 are the security gates and
must be explicit, not implicit.

