% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Beyond MVP --- post-v0.5.1, in shape only}
\label{sec:post-mvp}

The following is intentionally not specified in v0.5.1 and is
listed only to keep it out of the MVP code:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=0.15em]
    \item \textbf{SPA support.} \texttt{spa\_fallback} arg on
    publish; fetch returns \texttt{index.html} on path miss.
    \item \textbf{API surface.} Additional project-owned
    abilities under \texttt{ability/<user>.<project>.api.<verb>};
    Hub gains a parallel \texttt{/api/<verb>} route (which
    invokes the project-owned API ability rather than serving
    bytes).
    \item \textbf{Peer-agent action discovery.} Project-owned
    \texttt{actions.*} abilities for runtime intent discovery
    by other agents.
    \item \textbf{Visibility = PRIVATE / SCOPED.} Capability-URL
    semantics; \texttt{<project-id>} extends to a 64-hex
    content hash or a separately-minted unguessable token.
    \item \textbf{Federation.} Content-addressed
    \texttt{<project-id>} means a second host with the same
    bytes can serve the same URL; multi-host serve becomes
    natural.
    \item \textbf{Aliases.} \texttt{<user>.pages.alias} ability
    mapping a slug to a content hash; \texttt{<slug>.<user>.pages.<realm>}
    becomes a stable URL across content updates.
    \item \textbf{Daemon-restart persistence.}
    \texttt{PublishedProjects} survives restart by re-opening
    \texttt{folder\_fd}s from a persisted manifest.
\end{itemize}

Each is purely additive: the URA shape, the sandbox boundary,
the Hub-as-agent role, and the receipt tier classification all
stay constant. v0.5.1 establishes the addressing form once;
larger features compose over it.

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Open decisions}
\label{sec:open}

\begin{decisionbox}
\textbf{Hub binary placement (MVP vs production).} v0.5.1 lets
the daemon serve \texttt{*.*.pages.<realm>} on a local port for
MVP. Production needs the Hub in the backend (Go), where TLS,
wildcard certs, and shared cookie domain already exist. The
cut-over is mechanical: the Go backend registers
\texttt{ability/01HUB.pages.serve} doing the same
\texttt{forward\_invoke}; ontology unchanged.
\end{decisionbox}

\begin{decisionbox}
\textbf{macOS sandbox parity.} v0.5.1's macOS read sandbox is
weaker than Linux's \texttt{RESOLVE\_BENEATH}. Strengthening
paths: (a) per-publish sandboxed worker pinned to the folder via
\texttt{sandbox-exec}; (b) wait for an Apple equivalent of
\texttt{RESOLVE\_BENEATH}. Production targets Linux.
\end{decisionbox}

\begin{decisionbox}
\textbf{\texttt{<project-id>} length and policy.} v0.5.1
defaults to user-chosen names for PUBLIC. PRIVATE/SCOPED
publishes will require 64-hex content hashes or 256-bit tokens.
The chosen length must be recorded in the publish receipt so
verification cannot silently widen attack surface.
\end{decisionbox}

\begin{decisionbox}
\textbf{Wildcard subdomain TLS.} Production requires
\texttt{*.*.pages.easynet.run} (two-level wildcard).
Let's Encrypt issues two-level wildcards for
\texttt{pages.easynet.run} only with DNS-01 challenge. The
Hub deploy needs a DNS-01 ACME flow; this is operations work,
not protocol work.
\end{decisionbox}

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Coverage check}

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.18}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{l X l}
\toprule
\textbf{Rule} & \textbf{Discharged by} & \textbf{Where} \\
\midrule
URA v4.1.5 §A.URA-7 (3-segment URA) & The tail \texttt{<id-part>/<path-part>} is one URA segment & \S\ref{sec:ura} \\
URA v4.1.5 §9 (callee \(\in\) hub/device/agent) & Fetch callee is \texttt{agent/<host>}; Hub-serve callee is \texttt{hub/01HUB} & \S\ref{sec:hub} \\
ONTOLOGY §3.2 (skill = ability with visibility) & PRIVATE/SCOPED reuse the existing visibility enum & \S\ref{sec:post-mvp} \\
ONTOLOGY (ability has one owner) & Owner widened from agent to (user \(\mid\) agent \(\mid\) hub \(\mid\) resource); single owner per ability preserved & \S\ref{sec:ura} \\
AXIOM (every invocation produces a receipt) & Two-tier classification (canonical / operational) & \S\ref{sec:receipts} \\
forward\_invoke endgame contract & Hub uses the standard \texttt{forward\_invoke}; no parallel path & \S\ref{sec:hub} \\
Sandbox discipline (kernel-enforced) & PG-INV-4 (Linux \texttt{RESOLVE\_BENEATH}) & \S\ref{sec:sandbox} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}

\paragraph{Closing.} A URA that points at a public byte stream
is the simplest answer to ``where is this byte on EasyNet'':
the user-and-project name a sandboxed root, the path inside the
URA names the byte. Publishing mints the addressing; the Hub
translates HTTP into invocations of one project-owned ability;
the kernel keeps the read path inside the published root. The
MVP is two files, three days of code, seven test cases, and one
ontology decision restated: the URA tail is identity-then-path,
and the path part is real bytes inside the project's hard
sandbox boundary.

\vfill
\hrule
\vspace{0.4em}
\noindent\small
\textit{The URA is not a label on a thing. It is the address of
a byte. The thing is whatever the address resolves to inside a
sandbox the kernel guarantees.}

\end{document}
